WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.455
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.306 --> 00:00:05.180
Climate change has
actually already happened.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.970
It's not something
that we can prevent,

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.664
at least fully at this point.

00:00:08.664 --> 00:00:10.080
You saw this with
Hurricane Maria,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.160
and most recently with
Hurricane Michael,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.790
that we're just seeing more
intense and more frequent

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.590
giant storms.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.620
My question as a researcher is
how can we develop technologies

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.612
that help people adapt
to this new environment?

00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:25.570
Help people live in a
world that's already been

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:26.694
affected by climate change.

00:00:26.694 --> 00:00:27.979
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.470
What people don't realize
is that a lot of this debris

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.090
is actually incredibly
energy-dense.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.030
Just raw aluminum has 40
times the energy density

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:40.610
of lithium-ion and two times the
energy density of diesel fuel.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.210
So the idea is to be able
to take a part of your house

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.880
that was broken, or a part
of your car-- like a wheel

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.280
that you can't use anymore--
and converting that

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.250
into fuel that can be used to
provide water and electricity.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.310
So if your car was
destroyed by the hurricane,

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.560
I might cannibalize one wheel
of my car and my family of four

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.910
could have fresh water
for like three months.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.599
Normally, if you take aluminum
and you expose it to water,

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:05.699
nothing happens.

00:01:05.699 --> 00:01:09.970
And that's because there is a
protective oxide layer coating

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.560
the aluminum surface.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.770
Turns out that if you
take these same BBs

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.700
and you apply a minimal
coating of gallium and indium

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.680
in the right
percentages, it actually

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:25.690
becomes super exothermically
reactive with water.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.180
These little bubbles that
you're seeing are hydrogen.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.052
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.820
And actually at
this point I'm going

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.590
to stick it in the
fume hood here,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.730
just because it's getting
a bit too hot to hold.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.602
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.850
What I'm doing is
I'm using that energy

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.145
to desalinate water and
provide electricity.

00:01:45.145 --> 00:01:46.783
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.820
This is the desalinator.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.680
There are no extra
components needed.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.480
No electricity is required.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.790
It has essentially
no moving parts.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.900
This is an incredibly robust way
to produce clean water almost

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.689
immediately.

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:03.980
All of that hydrogen and
steam and heat that's produced

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.510
goes into generating really
high pressures in here.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.370
It's about 2,000 PSI.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.390
That pressure pushes
down this piston,

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.630
which forces seawater through
a semi-permeable membrane.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.230
Oh, wow.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.938
So it's a process
called reverse osmosis.

00:02:16.938 --> 00:02:18.290
Right.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.700
And then the really
cool thing is

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.530
that after I've desalinated
whatever volume of water

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.420
I want to desalinate, I
have all this hydrogen,

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.270
this pressurized
hydrogen, left over.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.720
So what I do is I just
open up this valve

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.280
and I divert the hydrogen
into a fuel cell.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.710
And now I have electricity.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:34.369
Wow.

00:02:34.369 --> 00:02:36.410
So there's still a ways
to go before you can just

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:40.830
take any old aluminum and
do what I'm saying here.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.030
But in a lab, it
works totally great.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

